-- Topic 1: Introduction to Agriculture
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Introduction to Agriculture',
   'Definition, branches, importance and history of agriculture in West Africa',
   E'## Introduction to Agriculture\n\n### Definition of Agriculture\nAgriculture is the science, art, and practice of cultivating the soil, growing crops, raising livestock, and preparing and marketing the resulting products. It encompasses all activities involved in the production of food, fiber, fuel, and other products through the systematic cultivation of plants and animals.\n\n### Branches of Agriculture\nAgriculture is divided into several major branches:\n\n**1. Crop Production (Agronomy)**\n- Growing of field crops such as maize, cassava, yam, and rice\n- Includes cereal crops, legumes, root and tuber crops\n\n**2. Horticulture**\n- Production of vegetables, fruits, and ornamental plants\n- Subdivided into: Olericulture (vegetables), Pomology (fruits), Floriculture (flowers)\n\n**3. Animal Husbandry (Zootechnics)**\n- Rearing of livestock: cattle, goats, sheep, pigs\n- Includes poultry farming, dairy farming, and bee-keeping\n\n**4. Fisheries**\n- Capture fisheries and aquaculture (fish farming)\n\n**5. Forestry**\n- Management of forest resources, timber production, and conservation\n\n**6. Agricultural Economics**\n- Farm management, marketing, and economics of agricultural production\n\n**7. Agricultural Engineering**\n- Design and use of farm tools, machinery, irrigation systems\n\n### Importance of Agriculture\nAgriculture plays a vital role in:\n\n| Sector | Role |\n|--------|------|\n| Food Security | Provides food for the population |\n| Employment | Major employer in West Africa (60-80% of workforce) |\n| Raw Materials | Supplies agro-industries with raw materials |\n| Foreign Exchange | Export of cocoa, coffee, timber generates income |\n| GDP Contribution | Contributes 20-40% of GDP in many African nations |\n\n### History of Agriculture in West Africa\n\n**Pre-Colonial Period**\n- Shifting cultivation was the main farming system\n- Crops like yam, millet, sorghum, and cowpea were cultivated\n- Animal domestication: cattle, goats, sheep, and poultry\n\n**Colonial Period (1800s–1960s)**\n- Introduction of cash crops: cocoa, coffee, groundnuts, cotton\n- Development of plantation agriculture\n- Introduction of new farming technologies\n\n**Post-Independence Period**\n- Government focus on food self-sufficiency\n- Green Revolution initiatives\n- Development of agricultural research institutions (CSIR in Ghana)\n\n### Types of Farming Systems\n\n**1. Subsistence Farming**\n- Producing just enough for family consumption\n- Small-scale, low capital input\n- Common in rural West Africa\n\n**2. Commercial Farming**\n- Large-scale production for sale\n- High capital, technology, and labor input\n- Examples: cocoa farms, rice plantations\n\n**3. Mixed Farming**\n- Combining crop cultivation with livestock rearing\n- Efficient use of farm resources\n\n**4. Shifting Cultivation**\n- Clearing land, farming for a few years, then moving on\n- Allows soil to regenerate naturally\n\n**5. Nomadic Herding**\n- Moving livestock from place to place for pasture\n- Common in Sahel regions\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know all branches of agriculture with examples\n- Distinguish between subsistence and commercial farming\n- Understand the role of agriculture in national development\n- Know major cash and food crops of West Africa',
   1, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 2: Soil Science and Soil Management
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000002-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Soil Science and Soil Management',
   'Soil formation, composition, properties, types and management practices',
   E'## Soil Science and Soil Management\n\n### What is Soil?\nSoil is the thin uppermost layer of the earth\'s crust that supports plant growth. It is a complex mixture of minerals, organic matter, water, air, and living organisms.\n\n### Soil Formation (Pedogenesis)\nSoil is formed through the weathering of parent rock material over thousands of years.\n\n**Factors of Soil Formation (CLORPT):**\n- **C** - Climate (rainfall, temperature)\n- **L** - Living organisms (plants, animals, microbes)\n- **O** - Organic matter\n- **R** - Relief/Topography\n- **P** - Parent material (bedrock)\n- **T** - Time\n\n### Soil Composition\nA typical fertile soil consists of:\n- **45%** Mineral matter (sand, silt, clay)\n- **5%** Organic matter (humus)\n- **25%** Water\n- **25%** Air\n\n### Soil Texture\nSoil texture refers to the relative proportions of sand, silt, and clay particles.\n\n| Particle | Size | Properties |\n|----------|------|------------|\n| Sand | 0.05–2.0 mm | Coarse, well-drained, low fertility |\n| Silt | 0.002–0.05 mm | Medium, moderate drainage |\n| Clay | < 0.002 mm | Fine, poorly drained, high fertility |\n\n**Loam** is a mixture of sand, silt, and clay — ideal for crop production.\n\n### Soil Structure\nSoil structure refers to the arrangement of soil particles into aggregates.\n\n**Types of soil structure:**\n- Granular (rounded aggregates) — found in topsoil, good for farming\n- Blocky (angular/subangular) — subsoil\n- Platy (flat layers) — impedes drainage\n- Prismatic/Columnar — subsoil, poor drainage\n- Single grain (no aggregation) — sandy soils\n\n### Soil Profile\nA vertical cross-section of soil showing different layers (horizons):\n\n| Horizon | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| O | Organic matter/leaf litter on surface |\n| A | Topsoil — dark, rich in humus, most biological activity |\n| B | Subsoil — leached materials from above |\n| C | Weathered parent material |\n| R | Bedrock |\n\n### Soil pH\n- **pH scale:** 0–14\n- **Acidic soils:** pH < 7 (pineapple, rubber prefer slightly acidic)\n- **Neutral:** pH = 7\n- **Alkaline/Basic:** pH > 7\n- Most crops grow best at pH **6.0–7.0**\n- **Liming** is used to correct acidic soils\n\n### Soil Drainage and Water Retention\n- **Well-drained soils:** Sandy soils — poor water retention\n- **Poorly drained soils:** Clay soils — waterlogging risk\n- **Capillarity:** Movement of water upward through soil pores\n\n### Soil Erosion\nSoil erosion is the removal of topsoil by water, wind, or human activity.\n\n**Types of Erosion:**\n- **Sheet erosion:** Uniform removal of topsoil\n- **Rill erosion:** Small channels formed by running water\n- **Gully erosion:** Large channels/gullies\n- **Wind erosion:** Removal by wind (common in savanna regions)\n\n**Causes:** Deforestation, overgrazing, improper farming, heavy rainfall\n\n### Soil Conservation Methods\n- **Mulching:** Covering soil with organic material\n- **Contour farming:** Ploughing across slopes\n- **Terracing:** Building steps on hillsides\n- **Cover cropping:** Growing plants to protect soil\n- **Windbreaks/Shelterbelts:** Rows of trees to reduce wind erosion\n- **Strip cropping:** Alternating strips of different crops\n- **Crop rotation:** Changing crops each season\n\n### Soil Management\n- **Tillage:** Ploughing, harrowing to prepare seedbed\n- **Liming:** Adding lime (CaCO₃) to reduce soil acidity\n- **Addition of organic matter:** Compost, farmyard manure\n- **Drainage:** Improving waterlogged soils\n- **Irrigation:** Supplying water to dry soils\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Describe the composition of a fertile soil\n- Know the soil texture triangle and particle sizes\n- Explain the soil profile and each horizon\n- List and explain soil erosion types and conservation methods\n- Understand soil pH and its effect on crop growth',
   2, 35)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 3: Plant Nutrients and Fertilizers
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000003-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Plant Nutrients and Fertilizers',
   'Essential plant nutrients, deficiency symptoms, and types of fertilizers',
   E'## Plant Nutrients and Fertilizers\n\n### Essential Plant Nutrients\nPlants require 17 essential nutrients for healthy growth, divided into macronutrients and micronutrients.\n\n**Macronutrients (needed in large amounts):**\n\n| Nutrient | Symbol | Function | Deficiency Symptom |\n|----------|--------|----------|--------------------|\n| Nitrogen | N | Leaf growth, chlorophyll | Yellowing (chlorosis) of older leaves |\n| Phosphorus | P | Root development, energy | Purple/red leaves, poor root growth |\n| Potassium | K | Disease resistance, water regulation | Scorching of leaf margins |\n| Calcium | Ca | Cell wall strength | Tip burn, blossom end rot |\n| Magnesium | Mg | Chlorophyll component | Interveinal chlorosis |\n| Sulphur | S | Protein synthesis | Yellowing of young leaves |\n\n**Micronutrients (needed in small amounts):**\n- Iron (Fe), Manganese (Mn), Zinc (Zn), Copper (Cu), Boron (B), Molybdenum (Mo), Chlorine (Cl)\n\n### The Role of NPK\n**N (Nitrogen):**\n- Promotes vegetative growth (leaves and stems)\n- Component of chlorophyll and amino acids\n- Deficiency: Pale yellow/green leaves starting from older leaves\n\n**P (Phosphorus):**\n- Essential for root development and fruit formation\n- Involved in energy transfer (ATP)\n- Deficiency: Purplish or reddish discoloration\n\n**K (Potassium):**\n- Regulates water uptake (osmosis)\n- Increases disease resistance and grain quality\n- Deficiency: Marginal scorch of leaves\n\n### Types of Fertilizers\n\n**1. Organic Fertilizers (Natural)**\nDerived from plant or animal materials:\n- **Farmyard Manure (FYM):** Dung + urine + straw\n- **Compost:** Decomposed organic waste\n- **Green Manure:** Ploughing in leguminous crops\n- **Bone Meal:** Ground animal bones (high phosphorus)\n- **Blood Meal:** Dried animal blood (high nitrogen)\n- **Wood Ash:** Contains potassium and calcium\n\n**Advantages of Organic Fertilizers:**\n- Improves soil structure\n- Long-lasting, slow release\n- Environmentally friendly\n- Adds humus to soil\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Slow-acting\n- Low nutrient concentration\n- Bulky and difficult to transport\n\n**2. Inorganic/Chemical Fertilizers (Artificial)**\nManufactured through industrial processes:\n\n| Fertilizer | Nutrient Content | Use |\n|-----------|-----------------|-----|\n| Urea | 46% N | Broad leaf growth |\n| Ammonium Sulphate | 21% N | Acidic soils |\n| Single Superphosphate | 16-20% P₂O₅ | Root development |\n| Triple Superphosphate | 46% P₂O₅ | High P needs |\n| Muriate of Potash (KCl) | 60% K₂O | Grain filling |\n| NPK (15:15:15) | Balanced N, P, K | General purpose |\n\n**Advantages of Inorganic Fertilizers:**\n- Fast-acting\n- High nutrient concentration\n- Easy to apply and measure\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Expensive\n- Can cause soil acidification\n- Environmental pollution (leaching, runoff)\n- Does not improve soil structure\n\n### Methods of Fertilizer Application\n- **Broadcasting:** Scattering uniformly over the field\n- **Band/Row placement:** Placing in rows near seed\n- **Top dressing:** Applying to growing crop surface\n- **Foliar spray:** Dissolving in water and spraying leaves\n- **Fertigation:** Applying through irrigation water\n\n### Factors Affecting Fertilizer Use\n- Soil pH\n- Crop type and stage of growth\n- Rainfall and moisture availability\n- Soil texture and fertility\n- Farmer\'s financial capacity\n\n### Nitrogen Cycle\nNitrogen moves through:\n1. **Nitrogen fixation:** N₂ → NH₃ (by bacteria like Rhizobium in legume nodules)\n2. **Nitrification:** NH₄⁺ → NO₂⁻ → NO₃⁻\n3. **Assimilation:** Plants absorb NO₃⁻\n4. **Denitrification:** NO₃⁻ → N₂ (back to atmosphere)\n5. **Decomposition:** Dead organisms → ammonium\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Distinguish between macro and micronutrients\n- Know deficiency symptoms of N, P, K\n- Compare organic vs inorganic fertilizers with advantages/disadvantages\n- Describe methods of fertilizer application\n- Explain the nitrogen cycle',
   3, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 4: Crop Production: Field Crops
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000004-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Crop Production: Field Crops',
   'Production practices for major field crops including maize, cassava, yam, rice and cowpea',
   E'## Crop Production: Field Crops\n\n### Classification of Crops\n\n**By Use:**\n- **Food crops:** Maize, cassava, yam, rice, cowpea\n- **Cash crops:** Cocoa, coffee, cotton, rubber\n- **Industrial crops:** Sugarcane, cotton, oil palm\n- **Cover crops:** Centrosema, Calopogonium (protect soil)\n\n**By Life Span:**\n- **Annuals:** Complete life cycle in one year (maize, cowpea)\n- **Biennials:** Complete in two years (onion)\n- **Perennials:** Live for many years (cocoa, oil palm, rubber)\n\n### Major Field Crops and Their Production\n\n## MAIZE (Zea mays)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 21–27°C\n- Rainfall: 500–1300 mm/year\n- Well-distributed rainfall\n\n**Soil Requirements:** Well-drained loamy soils, pH 5.8–7.0\n\n**Production Steps:**\n1. Land preparation (ploughing and harrowing)\n2. Planting: Direct sowing at 75 cm × 30 cm spacing\n3. Fertilizer application: Basal NPK + top-dress nitrogen\n4. Weeding: 3 and 6 weeks after planting\n5. Pest and disease control\n6. Harvesting: 90–120 days after planting\n\n**Uses:** Food (kenkey, banku, akple), animal feed, starch, ethanol\n\n## CASSAVA (Manihot esculenta)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 25–29°C\n- Rainfall: 1000–1500 mm/year\n- Drought tolerant\n\n**Propagation:** Vegetative — stem cuttings (25–30 cm long)\n\n**Production Steps:**\n1. Land preparation\n2. Planting cuttings at 1m × 1m spacing\n3. Weed control\n4. Harvesting: 9–24 months after planting\n\n**Uses:** Food (fufu, gari, tapioca), starch, animal feed\n\n## YAM (Dioscorea spp.)\n**Species:** White yam (D. rotundata), water yam (D. alata)\n\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 25–30°C\n- Rainfall: 1000–1500 mm\n- Well-drained fertile soils\n\n**Propagation:** Tuber setts (yam seed pieces)\n\n**Production Steps:**\n1. Mound preparation (1 m high mounds)\n2. Planting setts at top of mounds\n3. Staking (yam vines need support)\n4. Weeding and earthing up\n5. Harvesting: 6–9 months\n\n**Yam Barn:** Traditional storage structure\n\n## RICE (Oryza sativa)\n**Types of Cultivation:**\n- **Upland rice:** Grown on well-drained land\n- **Lowland/Swamp rice:** Grown in flooded fields\n\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 20–38°C\n- Rainfall: 1000–2000 mm/year\n- Paddy (swamp) rice needs standing water\n\n**Production Steps:**\n1. Seedbed preparation (nursery)\n2. Transplanting seedlings 2–3 weeks old\n3. Water management in paddy fields\n4. Fertilizer application\n5. Harvesting: 3–5 months\n\n## COWPEA (Vigna unguiculata)\n**Benefits:**\n- Nitrogen-fixing legume\n- Drought-tolerant\n- Multiple uses (food and fodder)\n\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 20–35°C\n- Rainfall: 500–1200 mm\n\n**Spacing:** 75 cm × 30 cm\n\n**Harvesting:** 60–90 days after planting\n\n### Crop Rotation\nRotating different crops in sequence to maintain soil fertility:\n- Year 1: Maize (heavy feeder)\n- Year 2: Legume (cowpea, groundnut — fixes N)\n- Year 3: Root crop (cassava, yam)\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Reduces pest and disease buildup\n- Improves soil fertility\n- Controls weeds\n- Reduces erosion\n\n### Intercropping\nGrowing two or more crops simultaneously:\n- Maize + Cowpea\n- Yam + Maize\n- Cassava + Groundnut\n\n**Benefits:** Better land use, risk reduction, increased yield\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know climatic requirements for each major crop\n- Describe production steps from land preparation to harvest\n- Distinguish between annual and perennial crops\n- Explain the benefits of crop rotation and intercropping\n- Know propagation methods (seed vs vegetative)',
   4, 35)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 5: Crop Production: Horticultural Crops
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000005-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Crop Production: Horticultural Crops',
   'Vegetables, fruits, and ornamental plant production and management',
   E'## Crop Production: Horticultural Crops\n\n### What is Horticulture?\nHorticulture is the branch of agriculture that deals with the cultivation of garden plants — vegetables, fruits, flowers, and ornamental plants.\n\n**Branches of Horticulture:**\n- **Olericulture:** Vegetable production\n- **Pomology:** Fruit production\n- **Floriculture:** Flower production\n- **Landscaping/Ornamental Horticulture:** Decorative plants\n\n### Vegetable Production (Olericulture)\n\n**Classification of Vegetables by Part Used:**\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| Root vegetables | Carrot, radish, turnip |\n| Leaf vegetables | Lettuce, cabbage, spinach |\n| Fruit vegetables | Tomato, pepper, cucumber |\n| Stem vegetables | Celery, asparagus |\n| Flower vegetables | Cauliflower, broccoli |\n\n### Tomato Production (Lycopersicon esculentum)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 18–27°C (fruit setting)\n- Rainfall: 600–1200 mm, well-distributed\n- Full sunlight\n\n**Soil:** Well-drained loamy soils, pH 5.5–6.8\n\n**Production Steps:**\n1. **Nursery preparation:** Sow seeds in nursery beds\n2. **Transplanting:** After 3–4 weeks (seedlings 15–20 cm tall)\n3. **Spacing:** 60 cm × 45 cm\n4. **Staking:** Support plants with stakes\n5. **Pruning:** Remove suckers to direct energy to fruits\n6. **Fertilizer:** Basal NPK; top-dress with nitrogen\n7. **Irrigation:** Regular watering\n8. **Harvesting:** 70–90 days after transplanting\n\n**Pests and Diseases:**\n- Pests: Whitefly, thrips, fruit borer\n- Diseases: Early blight, late blight, bacterial wilt\n\n### Pepper Production (Capsicum spp.)\n**Types:** Chili pepper, bell pepper, cayenne\n\n**Production similar to tomato:**\n- Temperature: 20–30°C\n- Spacing: 60 cm × 45 cm\n- Harvest: 90–120 days\n\n### Okra Production (Abelmoschus esculentus)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 25–35°C\n- Rainfall: 600–1200 mm\n- Drought tolerant\n\n**Direct sowing:** 60 cm × 30 cm\n\n**Harvest:** 50–65 days after planting\n\n### Fruit Production (Pomology)\n\n## MANGO (Mangifera indica)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 24–27°C\n- Rainfall: 750–1250 mm with dry season for flowering\n- Altitude: up to 1200 m\n\n**Propagation:** Seed, budding, grafting (vegetative preferred for true-to-type)\n\n**Spacing:** 10 m × 10 m\n\n**Harvesting:** 3–5 years (grafted) or 5–7 years (seedling)\n\n## CITRUS (Oranges, Lemon, Lime, Grapefruit)\n**Climatic Requirements:**\n- Temperature: 25–30°C\n- Rainfall: 1000–1500 mm\n- Well-drained deep soils\n\n**Propagation:** Budding and grafting (most common)\n\n**Spacing:** 6 m × 6 m for oranges\n\n**Harvesting:** 3–4 years after planting\n\n## PINEAPPLE (Ananas comosus)\n**Propagation:** Suckers, slips, ratoons, crowns\n\n**Spacing:** 60 cm × 30 cm in double rows\n\n**Flowering:** Induced with ethephon or acetylene\n\n**Harvest:** 12–18 months after planting\n\n### Nursery Management\nA nursery is where seedlings are raised before transplanting to the field.\n\n**Types of Nurseries:**\n- **Bare-root nursery:** Seedlings grown in open ground\n- **Container nursery:** Grown in polybags/pots\n- **Protected nursery:** In greenhouses or shade houses\n\n**Nursery Operations:**\n1. Preparation of nursery beds\n2. Seed treatment (scarification, soaking)\n3. Sowing seeds\n4. Watering and shade management\n5. Thinning and spacing\n6. Hardening off before transplanting\n\n### Post-Harvest Management\n**Why Post-Harvest Management?**\n- Reduce losses (up to 30–40% of produce is lost)\n- Maintain quality and freshness\n- Increase market value\n\n**Post-Harvest Technologies:**\n- **Sorting and grading:** Selecting by size and quality\n- **Cleaning and washing:** Remove dirt\n- **Pre-cooling:** Reduce temperature quickly\n- **Storage:** Cold storage, evaporative cooling\n- **Processing:** Canning, drying, juicing\n- **Packaging:** Protect from damage during transport\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know the branches of horticulture with examples\n- Describe tomato production from nursery to harvest\n- Compare vegetative vs seed propagation for fruits\n- Explain nursery management practices\n- Know post-harvest management methods',
   5, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 6: Crop Diseases and Pests
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000006-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Crop Diseases and Pests',
   'Major crop diseases, pests, symptoms, and control methods',
   E'## Crop Diseases and Pests\n\n### Crop Diseases\nA crop disease is any departure from normal growth or health of a plant caused by biological, physical, or chemical agents.\n\n**Classification of Crop Diseases:**\n\n**1. Fungal Diseases (most common)**\n| Disease | Crop Affected | Symptoms | Control |\n|---------|--------------|----------|--------|\n| Black pod | Cocoa | Black lesions on pods | Remove infected pods, copper fungicides |\n| Late blight | Tomato, potato | Dark spots, white mold | Metalaxyl fungicide |\n| Maize smut | Maize | Grey-black galls on ears | Resistant varieties, seed treatment |\n| Rice blast | Rice | Diamond-shaped lesions | Tricyclazole fungicide |\n| Leaf rust | Wheat, sorghum | Orange-brown pustules | Resistant varieties |\n\n**2. Bacterial Diseases**\n| Disease | Crop Affected | Symptoms |\n|---------|--------------|----------|\n| Bacterial wilt | Tomato, pepper | Sudden wilting, slime test positive |\n| Angular leaf spot | Cotton | Angular water-soaked spots |\n| Crown gall | Various | Tumor-like growths at crown |\n\n**3. Viral Diseases**\n| Disease | Crop Affected | Symptoms |\n|---------|--------------|----------|\n| Cassava mosaic virus | Cassava | Yellow-green mosaic pattern on leaves |\n| Maize streak virus | Maize | Yellow streaks on leaves |\n| Groundnut rosette | Groundnut | Stunting, mosaic yellowing |\n\n**4. Nematode Diseases**\n- Root-knot nematode (Meloidogyne spp.)\n- Affects roots of most crops\n- Symptoms: Galls/knots on roots, stunted growth\n- Control: Crop rotation, soil fumigants, resistant varieties\n\n### Crop Pests\nCrop pests are organisms that damage crops and reduce yield.\n\n**Classification by Feeding Habit:**\n\n**Chewing Insects (eat plant tissue)**\n| Pest | Crop | Damage |\n|------|------|--------|\n| Armyworm | Maize, sorghum | Defoliates leaves |\n| Locust | Many crops | Complete defoliation |\n| Pod borer | Cowpea | Bores into pods |\n| Cotton bollworm | Cotton | Damages bolls |\n\n**Sucking Insects (suck plant sap)**\n| Pest | Crop | Damage |\n|------|------|--------|\n| Aphids | Many crops | Suck sap, transmit viruses |\n| Whiteflies | Tomato, cassava | Suck sap, spread viruses |\n| Mealybugs | Cassava, cocoa | Suck sap, coat in waxy substance |\n| Stink bugs | Beans, rice | Puncture seeds |\n\n**Stem Borers**\n- Maize stalk borer (Busseola fusca)\n- Dead heart (young plants) and ear damage\n- Control: Pesticides, parasitoid wasps\n\n**Storage Pests**\n| Pest | Crop | Damage |\n|------|------|--------|\n| Weevils (Sitophilus) | Maize, rice | Bore into grains |\n| Bean beetle (Callosobruchus) | Cowpea | Larvae inside seeds |\n| Grain moth | Sorghum, millet | Larvae feed on grain |\n\n### Vertebrate Pests\n- **Rodents:** Rats, mice — damage crops in field and storage\n- **Birds:** Quelea quelea (most destructive bird pest in Africa)\n- **Monkeys and baboons:** Damage maize and fruit crops\n- **Hippopotamus:** Damage rice crops near water bodies\n\n### Methods of Pest and Disease Control\n\n**1. Cultural Control**\n- Crop rotation — breaks pest/disease cycles\n- Proper spacing — reduces humidity, improves air circulation\n- Timely planting — avoid peak pest seasons\n- Removal of crop debris — destroys pest habitat\n- Use of resistant varieties\n\n**2. Biological Control**\n- Using natural enemies to control pests\n- Predators: Ladybirds eat aphids\n- Parasitoids: Wasps parasitize caterpillars\n- Pathogens: Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) kills insects\n\n**3. Chemical Control (Pesticides)**\n- **Insecticides:** Kill insects (DDT, malathion, cypermethrin)\n- **Fungicides:** Kill fungi (mancozeb, metalaxyl, copper oxychloride)\n- **Herbicides:** Kill weeds\n- **Nematicides:** Kill nematodes\n- **Rodenticides:** Kill rodents\n\n**Advantages:** Fast-acting, highly effective\n**Disadvantages:** Environmental pollution, kills beneficial insects, resistance development, expensive\n\n**4. Integrated Pest Management (IPM)**\nCombines multiple control methods:\n- Cultural + Biological + Chemical\n- Monitors pest populations\n- Uses pesticides only when necessary (economic threshold)\n- Environmentally sustainable\n\n### Safety in Pesticide Use\n- Wear protective clothing (gloves, mask, goggles)\n- Read and follow label instructions\n- Store safely away from food and children\n- Dispose of empty containers properly\n- Observe pre-harvest intervals\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Classify diseases by causative agent (fungi, bacteria, virus)\n- Know major pests of common crops and their damage\n- Explain cultural, biological, and chemical control methods\n- Describe Integrated Pest Management (IPM)\n- Know safety precautions for pesticide application',
   6, 35)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 7: Weed Control in Agriculture
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000007-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Weed Control in Agriculture',
   'Types of weeds, their effects on crops, and methods of weed control',
   E'## Weed Control in Agriculture\n\n### What is a Weed?\nA weed is a plant growing where it is not wanted. Weeds are plants that compete with cultivated crops for resources.\n\n### Classification of Weeds\n\n**By Life Span:**\n- **Annual weeds:** Complete life cycle in one year (Amaranthus, goosegrass)\n- **Biennial weeds:** Complete in two years\n- **Perennial weeds:** Live for many years (Imperata cylindrica — spear grass, nutgrass)\n\n**By Morphology:**\n- **Grasses:** Narrow leaves, parallel veins (goosegrass, Imperata)\n- **Sedges:** Triangular stems, grass-like (nutgrass — Cyperus rotundus)\n- **Broadleaved weeds:** Wide leaves, netted veins (Amaranthus, Chromolaena)\n\n### Characteristics of Successful Weeds\n- Produce large numbers of seeds\n- Seeds remain viable for long periods\n- Rapid germination and growth\n- Adapt to various environments\n- Vegetative reproduction\n\n### Harmful Effects of Weeds\n1. **Competition:** Compete with crops for water, light, nutrients, space\n2. **Reduced yield:** Can reduce crop yield by 20–80%\n3. **Harbour pests and diseases:** Serve as alternate hosts\n4. **Impede mechanization:** Clog machinery\n5. **Allelopathy:** Some weeds release chemicals that inhibit crop growth\n6. **Increase production costs:** More labour needed\n7. **Poisonous weeds:** Toxic to livestock (Lantana camara)\n\n### Beneficial Effects of Weeds\n- Protect soil from erosion\n- Provide organic matter when decomposed\n- Some are edible (Amaranthus leaves)\n- Provide pasture for livestock\n\n### Major Weeds in West Africa\n| Weed | Scientific Name | Type |\n|------|----------------|------|\n| Spear grass | Imperata cylindrica | Grass |\n| Nutgrass/Purple nutsedge | Cyperus rotundus | Sedge |\n| Siam weed | Chromolaena odorata | Broadleaf |\n| Goosegrass | Eleusine indica | Grass |\n| Wild poinsettia | Euphorbia heterophylla | Broadleaf |\n| Striga | Striga hermonthica | Parasitic |\n\n**Striga (Witchweed)** is a parasitic weed that attaches to roots of maize and sorghum, very destructive.\n\n### Methods of Weed Control\n\n**1. Manual/Hand Weeding**\n- Using hands, hoe, cutlass\n- Most common in subsistence farming\n- Labour intensive but effective and precise\n- Best done when weeds are young (2–3 weeks after planting)\n\n**2. Mechanical/Cultural Control**\n- **Tillage:** Ploughing disrupts and buries weeds\n- **Crop rotation:** Different crops suppress different weeds\n- **Mulching:** Covering soil with organic material suppresses weeds\n- **Dense planting/Close spacing:** Canopy shades out weeds\n- **Flooding:** In rice paddies, floods suppress most weeds\n\n**3. Chemical Control (Herbicides)**\nHerbicides are chemicals used to kill or suppress weeds.\n\n**Classification by Action:**\n- **Selective herbicides:** Kill specific weeds without harming crops\n  - Atrazine: Controls broadleaf weeds in maize\n  - 2,4-D: Controls broadleaved weeds in grasses\n- **Non-selective herbicides:** Kill all plants\n  - Glyphosate (Roundup): Kills all vegetation\n  - Paraquat: Contact herbicide, burns all green plant tissue\n\n**Classification by Application Time:**\n- **Pre-emergence herbicides:** Applied before weed germination\n- **Post-emergence herbicides:** Applied after weeds emerge\n- **Pre-planting herbicides:** Applied before planting\n\n**Advantages of Herbicides:**\n- Fast, efficient, economical for large farms\n- Can reach areas inaccessible by hand\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Environmental pollution, groundwater contamination\n- Can harm non-target plants\n- Risk to farmer health if misused\n- Weed resistance can develop\n\n**4. Biological Control**\n- Using insects, fungi, or other organisms to control weeds\n- Example: Zygogramma beetle controls Parthenium weed\n- Grass carp (fish) control aquatic weeds\n\n**5. Integrated Weed Management (IWM)**\nCombination of multiple control methods for sustainable weed management.\n\n### Critical Weed Period\n- The period when crop yield is most sensitive to weed competition\n- Usually the first 3–6 weeks after planting\n- Keeping crops weed-free during this period prevents significant yield loss\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define a weed and classify by life span and morphology\n- List and explain harmful effects of weeds\n- Name major weeds in West Africa with scientific names\n- Distinguish between selective and non-selective herbicides\n- Compare manual, cultural, and chemical weed control methods',
   7, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 8: Farm Tools and Machinery
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000008-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Farm Tools and Machinery',
   'Types of farm tools and machines, their uses, maintenance and farm power sources',
   E'## Farm Tools and Machinery\n\n### Classification of Farm Tools\n\n**Simple/Hand Tools:**\nUsed manually without mechanical power.\n\n| Tool | Use |\n|------|-----|\n| Cutlass/Machete | Clearing vegetation, harvesting |\n| Hoe | Weeding, mounding, tillage |\n| Shovel/Spade | Digging, transplanting |\n| Rake | Levelling, removing debris |\n| Hand fork | Loosening soil, transplanting |\n| Watering can | Irrigating nurseries and small gardens |\n| Wheelbarrow | Transporting materials |\n| Dibber | Making holes for planting |\n| Pruning shears | Pruning branches |\n| Axe | Cutting trees, clearing |\n\n**Maintenance of Hand Tools:**\n- Clean after use to prevent rusting\n- Sharpen cutting edges regularly\n- Oil metal parts to prevent rust\n- Store in a dry, covered place\n- Replace broken or worn handles\n\n### Farm Machinery\nMachines used to perform agricultural operations, powered by engines.\n\n**Tractor and Tractor-Drawn Implements:**\n\n**The Tractor:**\n- Most versatile farm machine\n- Powers various implements through power take-off (PTO)\n- Types: Two-wheel drive and four-wheel drive\n\n**Primary Tillage Implements:**\n| Implement | Function |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Mouldboard plough | Turns over soil slices, buries crop residues |\n| Disc plough | Cuts through tough soils and dry conditions |\n| Chisel plough | Sub-soil loosening without inverting soil |\n\n**Secondary Tillage Implements:**\n| Implement | Function |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Disc harrow | Breaks clods, levels soil |\n| Rotary tiller/Rotavator | Fine seedbed preparation |\n| Roller | Compresses soil, crushes clods |\n\n**Planting Equipment:**\n- **Seed drill:** Plants seeds in rows at controlled depth and spacing\n- **Fertilizer spreader:** Broadcasts fertilizer uniformly\n- **Transplanter:** For rice and vegetable transplanting\n\n**Harvesting Machinery:**\n- **Combine harvester:** Simultaneously cuts, threshes, and cleans grain\n- **Maize sheller:** Removes maize grains from cobs\n- **Thresher:** Separates grain from straw\n- **Groundnut digger:** Lifts groundnut plants\n\n**Crop Processing Machinery:**\n- **Grain mill:** Grinds grain into flour\n- **Rice huller/polisher:** Removes rice husk\n- **Oil press:** Extracts oil from palm fruit, groundnut\n- **Gari fryer:** Fries gari (from cassava)\n\n### Sources of Farm Power\n\n**1. Human Power (Animate Power)**\n- Most common in subsistence farming\n- Limited capacity and efficiency\n- Tools: hoe, cutlass, shovel\n\n**2. Animal Power**\n- Using animals (oxen, donkeys, horses) to pull implements\n- Ox-drawn plough common in northern Ghana, Nigeria\n- More efficient than human power\n- Implements: plough, harrow, cart\n\n**3. Mechanical Power**\n- Tractors, engines, motors\n- Most efficient for large-scale farming\n- Types: Petrol engines, diesel engines\n\n**4. Electrical Power**\n- Electric motors for water pumps, milking machines, grain mills\n- Requires reliable electricity supply\n\n**5. Solar Power (Renewable)**\n- Solar pumps for irrigation\n- Solar drying of produce\n- Environmentally friendly, low maintenance\n\n### Farm Mechanization\n**Benefits:**\n- Increases efficiency and output\n- Reduces drudgery and labour costs\n- Enables timely operations\n- Increases area under cultivation\n\n**Challenges in West Africa:**\n- High initial cost of machinery\n- Lack of spare parts and mechanics\n- Poor infrastructure (roads)\n- Small farm sizes — machines not economical\n- Inadequate technical training\n\n### Maintenance of Farm Machinery\n**Daily Maintenance:**\n- Check oil and fuel levels\n- Clean dust and debris\n- Check tire pressure\n- Check coolant level\n\n**Periodic Maintenance:**\n- Change engine oil and filter\n- Sharpen and replace worn parts\n- Grease all moving parts\n- Check electrical systems\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Identify and state the uses of common farm tools\n- Classify farm machinery by function (tillage, planting, harvesting)\n- Describe sources of farm power with advantages\n- Explain challenges of farm mechanization in Africa\n- Know maintenance practices for tools and machinery',
   8, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 9: Animal Husbandry: Livestock Production
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000009-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Animal Husbandry: Livestock Production',
   'Production systems and management of cattle, goats, sheep and pigs',
   E'## Animal Husbandry: Livestock Production\n\n### Definition\nAnimal husbandry is the branch of agriculture concerned with the rearing, care, and management of domesticated animals for various products and services.\n\n### Importance of Livestock Production\n- **Food:** Meat, milk, eggs provide protein\n- **Income:** Sale of animals and products\n- **Draft power:** Oxen for ploughing\n- **Manure:** Organic fertilizer for crops\n- **Hides and skins:** Leather production\n- **Social/Cultural:** Bride price, festivals\n\n### Classification of Livestock\n\n| Animal | Class | Products |\n|--------|-------|----------|\n| Cattle | Ruminant | Meat, milk, hides, draft |\n| Goat | Ruminant | Meat, milk, skin |\n| Sheep | Ruminant | Meat, wool, milk |\n| Pig | Monogastric | Pork, lard, skin |\n| Rabbit | Monogastric | Meat, skin |\n\n### CATTLE PRODUCTION\n\n**Breeds:**\n- **Beef breeds:** Hereford, Angus, Ndama (trypanotolerant)\n- **Dairy breeds:** Holstein-Friesian, Jersey, Guernsey\n- **Dual-purpose:** Shorthorn, Simmental\n\n**Systems of Management:**\n\n**Extensive System:**\n- Animals roam freely over large areas\n- Low input, low output\n- Common in northern Ghana and Sahel\n- Nomadic and semi-nomadic herding\n\n**Intensive System:**\n- Animals kept in confined housing (zero-grazing)\n- High input (feed, veterinary care)\n- High output per animal\n- Common in commercial dairy farming\n\n**Semi-Intensive System:**\n- Combination of grazing and supplementary feeding\n- Animals housed at night, graze during day\n\n**Cattle Housing:**\n- Needs shade, ventilation, clean water\n- Floor: Concrete (easy to clean)\n- Feeding and water troughs\n\n**Breeding:**\n- **Natural mating:** Bull with cows\n- **Artificial insemination (AI):** Superior semen used\n- Heat/Estrus detection is crucial\n- Gestation period: ~280 days (9 months)\n\n**Common Cattle Diseases:**\n- Foot-and-mouth disease (viral)\n- Rinderpest (now eradicated)\n- Trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness — tsetse fly)\n- Bovine tuberculosis\n\n### GOAT PRODUCTION\n\n**Breeds in West Africa:**\n- West African Dwarf goat\n- Sahelian goat (long-legged)\n- Boer goat (exotic, meat)\n\n**Management:**\n- Hardy, adaptable, low-input animals\n- Should be housed/tethered to prevent crop damage\n- Supplementary feeding of concentrates\n\n**Reproduction:**\n- Gestation: 150 days (5 months)\n- 1–3 kids per birth\n\n### SHEEP PRODUCTION\n\n**Breeds:**\n- Djallonke (West African Dwarf sheep) — resistant to trypanosomiasis\n- Sahelian (Fulani) sheep — large body, long legs\n\n**Wool sheep** are less common in tropical Africa due to heat.\n\n**Management similar to goats**\n\n**Gestation:** 147–155 days\n\n### PIG PRODUCTION\n\n**Breeds:**\n- **Exotic:** Large White, Landrace, Duroc\n- **Local:** West African pig (hardy but slow-growing)\n\n**Advantages of Pig Farming:**\n- High feed conversion efficiency\n- Large litter size (8–12 piglets)\n- Short gestation (114 days — 3 months, 3 weeks, 3 days)\n- Fast growth rate\n\n**Management:**\n- Pigs are omnivores — eat plant and animal material\n- Require proper housing (piggery) with cool conditions\n- Wallowing area or showering for temperature regulation\n\n**Pork products:** Pork chops, bacon, ham, lard, sausages\n\n### Livestock Records\nImportant records in livestock production:\n- Birth records\n- Weight records (weight gains)\n- Health/vaccination records\n- Production records (milk, eggs)\n- Feed consumption records\n- Breeding records\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Compare ruminants vs monogastrics\n- Explain extensive, intensive, and semi-intensive systems\n- Know breeds of cattle (beef vs dairy)\n- Describe reproductive characteristics of major livestock\n- Explain the importance of record keeping in animal production',
   9, 35)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 10: Poultry Production
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000010-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Poultry Production',
   'Types of poultry, housing systems, feeding, health management and egg production',
   E'## Poultry Production\n\n### Types of Poultry\n| Bird | Products | Notes |\n|------|----------|-------|\n| Chicken | Eggs, meat | Most common |\n| Turkey | Meat | Festive season |\n| Duck | Meat, eggs | Water birds |\n| Guinea fowl | Meat, eggs | Hardy, West African native |\n| Geese | Meat, feathers | |\n| Pigeon | Meat | |\n\n### Classification of Chickens by Purpose\n\n**Egg Layers (Layers):**\n- Breeds: White Leghorn, Rhode Island Red, ISA Brown\n- Start laying at 18–22 weeks\n- Production: 250–300 eggs/year\n- Small body size, efficient feed conversion for eggs\n\n**Broilers (Meat Birds):**\n- Breeds: Arbor Acres, Ross 308, Cobb 500\n- Market weight: 2–2.5 kg in 6–8 weeks\n- High feed conversion for rapid weight gain\n\n**Dual Purpose:**\n- Suitable for both eggs and meat\n- New Hampshire, Sussex breeds\n\n**Indigenous/Local Breeds:**\n- Small body size, low production\n- Hardy and disease resistant\n- Scavenge for food\n\n### Poultry Housing Systems\n\n**1. Extensive/Free-Range System**\n- Birds allowed to roam freely\n- Natural foraging for food\n- Low input, low output\n- Common for local/indigenous breeds\n- Advantages: Low cost, natural behavior, good welfare\n- Disadvantages: Low production, predator risk, disease spread\n\n**2. Semi-Intensive System**\n- Birds have access to outdoor runs\n- Housed at night\n- Better management than free-range\n\n**3. Intensive System**\n\n**Deep Litter System:**\n- Birds kept on deep layer of litter (wood shavings, rice husks)\n- Litter depth: 10–15 cm\n- Litter absorbs droppings\n- Advantages: Low labour, birds comfortable\n\n**Battery Cage System:**\n- Individual or small group cages in rows\n- Standard in commercial egg production\n- Advantages: High production, easy management, disease control\n- Disadvantages: High initial cost, animal welfare concerns\n\n**Broiler House:**\n- High stocking density\n- Climate control (ventilation, temperature)\n- All-in-all-out management\n\n### Brooding Management\n**Brooding** is the care of day-old chicks until they can regulate their own temperature (about 6 weeks).\n\n**Brooder Requirements:**\n- Heat source: Infra-red lamp, gas brooder, electric brooder\n- Temperature: 35°C first week, reduce by 3°C each week\n- Adequate space: 0.05 m²/chick\n- Drinkers and feeders: 1 per 50 chicks\n- Litter: Clean and dry\n\n**Signs of Correct Temperature:**\n- Chicks spread evenly → Temperature correct\n- Chicks huddle under lamp → Too cold\n- Chicks move away from lamp → Too hot\n- Chicks to one side → Draught\n\n### Poultry Feeding\n\n**Feed Types by Age:**\n| Stage | Feed Type | Protein % |\n|-------|-----------|----------|\n| Chicks (0–8 weeks) | Starter mash | 20–22% |\n| Growers (8–18 weeks) | Grower mash | 16–18% |\n| Layers (18+ weeks) | Layer mash | 15–17% |\n| Broilers (all) | Broiler starter/finisher | 22–24% |\n\n**Feed Ingredients:**\n- **Energy:** Maize, sorghum\n- **Protein:** Soybean meal, fishmeal, groundnut cake\n- **Minerals:** Bone meal, oyster shells (calcium for egg shells)\n- **Vitamins:** Premix supplement\n\n### Egg Production and Quality\n**Factors Affecting Egg Production:**\n- Breed (genetics)\n- Age of bird\n- Nutrition\n- Light (16 hours light/day stimulates laying)\n- Health status\n- Temperature\n\n**Egg Quality:**\n- Shell: Strong, clean, without cracks\n- Albumen (white): Thick and clear\n- Yolk: Round, firm, bright orange-yellow\n- Air cell: Small and intact\n\n### Poultry Diseases and Control\n| Disease | Cause | Symptoms | Control |\n|---------|-------|----------|--------|\n| Newcastle disease | Virus | Twisting neck, gasping | Vaccination |\n| Gumboro (IBD) | Virus | Bloody diarrhea, depression | Vaccination |\n| Marek\'s disease | Virus | Paralysis of legs/wings | Vaccination |\n| Coccidiosis | Protozoa | Bloody diarrhea | Coccidiostats in feed |\n| Fowl pox | Virus | Scabs on skin, mouth | Vaccination |\n\n**Vaccination Schedule:**\n- Day 1: Marek\'s disease\n- Day 7–10: Gumboro\n- Day 14–16: Newcastle disease\n- Day 21: Gumboro booster\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Distinguish between layers and broilers\n- Describe battery cage vs deep litter systems\n- Explain brooding management and temperature requirements\n- Know major poultry diseases and their vaccines\n- Describe factors affecting egg production',
   10, 35)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 11: Animal Nutrition and Feeding
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000011-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Animal Nutrition and Feeding',
   'Nutrients required by animals, feed types, and formulation of animal rations',
   E'## Animal Nutrition and Feeding\n\n### Importance of Animal Nutrition\nProper nutrition is essential for:\n- Growth and development\n- Reproduction and milk production\n- Resistance to disease\n- Overall health and productivity\n\n### Classes of Animal Nutrients\n\n**1. Carbohydrates**\n- Major energy source\n- Sources: Maize, cassava, sorghum, molasses\n- Functions: Energy for movement, body warmth, milk production\n\n**2. Proteins**\n- Body building and repair\n- Sources: Soybean meal, fishmeal, groundnut cake, blood meal\n- Functions: Growth, enzyme production, tissue repair, milk protein\n- Protein content: Crude Protein (CP) expressed as % in feed\n\n**3. Fats and Oils (Lipids)**\n- Concentrated energy (2.25x more than carbohydrates)\n- Sources: Palm kernel meal, copra meal, tallow\n- Functions: Energy, fat-soluble vitamins absorption, cell membranes\n\n**4. Vitamins**\n| Vitamin | Type | Function | Deficiency |\n|---------|------|----------|------------|\n| A | Fat-soluble | Vision, immunity | Night blindness |\n| D | Fat-soluble | Calcium metabolism | Rickets |\n| E | Fat-soluble | Reproduction, antioxidant | Poor fertility |\n| K | Fat-soluble | Blood clotting | Haemorrhage |\n| B-complex | Water-soluble | Energy metabolism | Various deficiencies |\n| C | Water-soluble | Immunity, collagen | Scurvy (rare in livestock) |\n\n**5. Minerals**\n\n**Macrominerals (needed in large amounts):**\n| Mineral | Function | Deficiency |\n|---------|----------|------------|\n| Calcium (Ca) | Bone, teeth, egg shell | Rickets, milk fever |\n| Phosphorus (P) | Bone, energy | Poor growth, bone weakness |\n| Sodium (Na) | Fluid balance | Loss of appetite |\n| Potassium (K) | Nerve function | Muscle weakness |\n| Magnesium (Mg) | Enzyme function | Grass tetany in cattle |\n\n**Microminerals (trace elements):**\n- Iron, Zinc, Copper, Iodine, Selenium, Cobalt\n\n**6. Water**\n- Most essential nutrient\n- Functions: Digestion, temperature regulation, waste removal\n- Daily requirement: Cattle need 40–70 L/day; poultry 200–300 ml/day\n\n### Types of Animal Feed\n\n**1. Roughages (High fiber, low energy)**\n- **Fresh pasture/Grass:** Elephant grass (Pennisetum purpureum), Guinea grass\n- **Hay:** Dried grass or legume forage\n- **Silage:** Fermented green forage stored anaerobically\n- **Crop residues:** Maize stover, rice straw, groundnut haulms\n\n**Silage Making:**\n1. Harvest forage at correct stage\n2. Chop into small pieces\n3. Pack tightly in silo/pit\n4. Cover and seal to exclude air (anaerobic)\n5. Fermentation produces lactic acid — preserves feed\n\n**2. Concentrates (Low fiber, high energy/protein)**\n- **Energy concentrates:** Maize, sorghum, cassava chips, molasses\n- **Protein concentrates:** Soybean meal, groundnut cake, fishmeal, blood meal\n\n**3. Supplements**\n- Mineral licks (salt blocks)\n- Vitamin-mineral premixes\n- Urea (non-protein nitrogen for ruminants)\n\n### Digestive Systems\n\n**Ruminants (Cattle, Sheep, Goats):**\n- 4-compartment stomach: Rumen, Reticulum, Omasum, Abomasum\n- **Rumen:** Fermentation chamber — microbes break down cellulose\n- **Rumination (cud chewing):** Re-chewing of feed for further breakdown\n- Can digest high-fiber roughages efficiently\n\n**Monogastrics (Pigs, Poultry):**\n- Simple single-stomach digestive system\n- Cannot efficiently digest high-fiber feeds\n- Require high-quality, easily digestible feeds\n\n### Feed Ration Formulation\nA **ration** is the total amount of feed given to an animal per day.\n\n**Principles of Ration Formulation:**\n- Meet nutrient requirements (Digestible Crude Protein, Metabolizable Energy)\n- Use locally available, affordable ingredients\n- Balance nutrients (Pearson Square method)\n- Ensure palatability\n\n**Pearson Square Method:**\nUsed to mix two ingredients to achieve a target protein level.\n\nExample: Mix soybean meal (44% CP) and maize (9% CP) to get 18% CP:\n- Difference from target: Maize part = 44 - 18 = 26\n- Soybean part = 18 - 9 = 9\n- Total parts = 35\n- Maize proportion = 26/35 = 74.3%\n- Soybean proportion = 9/35 = 25.7%\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know all 6 classes of nutrients with functions and sources\n- Distinguish roughages from concentrates\n- Explain the ruminant digestive system (4 chambers)\n- Describe silage making process\n- Know vitamin deficiency diseases in animals\n- Apply the Pearson Square for simple ration formulation',
   11, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 12: Animal Diseases and Parasites
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000012-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Animal Diseases and Parasites',
   'Major livestock diseases, parasites, symptoms, prevention and treatment',
   E'## Animal Diseases and Parasites\n\n### Classification of Animal Diseases\n\n**By Cause:**\n- **Infectious diseases:** Caused by pathogens (bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa)\n- **Non-infectious diseases:** Nutritional deficiencies, metabolic disorders, injuries\n- **Parasitic diseases:** Caused by external and internal parasites\n\n### Major Infectious Diseases\n\n**Bacterial Diseases:**\n| Disease | Animal Affected | Symptoms | Treatment |\n|---------|----------------|----------|----------|\n| Tuberculosis | Cattle | Weight loss, coughing | Culling (no treatment) |\n| Brucellosis | Cattle, goats | Abortion, infertility | Antibiotics, vaccination |\n| Anthrax | Cattle, sheep | Sudden death, swollen lymph nodes | Antibiotics, vaccination |\n| Blackleg | Cattle | Lameness, gas gangrene in leg | Vaccination (prevention) |\n\n**Viral Diseases:**\n| Disease | Animal Affected | Symptoms | Control |\n|---------|----------------|----------|--------|\n| Foot-and-mouth disease | Cattle, pigs | Blisters on mouth and feet | Vaccination, quarantine |\n| African Swine Fever | Pigs | High fever, haemorrhage, death | No vaccine, slaughter |\n| Rabies | All mammals | Aggression, paralysis, death | Vaccination (pre-exposure) |\n| Contagious bovine pleuropneumonia (CBPP) | Cattle | Lung pneumonia | Vaccination |\n\n**Protozoal Diseases:**\n| Disease | Vector | Animals | Symptoms |\n|---------|--------|---------|----------|\n| Trypanosomiasis | Tsetse fly | Cattle, pigs | Anaemia, weight loss, death |\n| East Coast Fever | Tick | Cattle | Swollen lymph nodes, fever |\n| Babesiosis | Tick | Cattle | Haemolytic anaemia, red urine |\n| Coccidiosis | No vector | Poultry, cattle | Bloody diarrhea |\n\n### Parasites of Livestock\n\n**External Parasites (Ectoparasites):**\n\n**Ticks:**\n- Most economically important external parasite\n- Vectors of East Coast Fever, Babesiosis, Anaplasmosis\n- Control: Dipping or spraying with acaricides (tickicides)\n\n**Tsetse Fly (Glossina spp.):**\n- Transmits Trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness)\n- Found in forest and riverine areas\n- Control: Traps, insecticide-impregnated targets, trypano-tolerant breeds\n\n**Lice:**\n- Cause itching, skin irritation, anaemia\n- Transmitted by direct contact\n- Control: Pour-on insecticides, dipping\n\n**Mites:**\n- Cause mange (scabies) — intense itching, hair loss\n- Types: Sarcoptic, Psoroptic, Demodectic mange\n- Control: Dipping, topical acaricides\n\n**Flies:**\n- Blow flies (cause myiasis/fly strike in wounds)\n- Horn flies, stable flies (blood-sucking)\n- Control: Wound care, insecticides\n\n**Internal Parasites (Endoparasites):**\n\n**Helminth Worms:**\n| Worm | Location | Animal | Symptoms |\n|------|----------|--------|----------|\n| Roundworms (Ascaris) | Intestine | Pigs, cattle | Weight loss, pot belly |\n| Tapeworms (Taenia) | Intestine | Cattle, pigs | Weight loss, cyst in muscles |\n| Liver fluke (Fasciola) | Liver | Cattle, sheep | Weight loss, anaemia, liver damage |\n| Lungworms (Dictyocaulus) | Lungs | Cattle, sheep | Coughing, respiratory distress |\n| Stomach worms (Haemonchus) | Abomasum | Sheep, goats | Anaemia, bottle jaw |\n\n**Bottle Jaw:**\nAccumulation of fluid under the jaw — sign of severe anaemia from bloodsucking worms.\n\n**Control of Internal Parasites:**\n- Anthelmintics (dewormers): Ivermectin, Albendazole, Levamisole\n- Regular deworming schedule\n- Rotational grazing\n- Good pasture management\n\n### Disease Prevention and Control\n\n**Prevention:**\n1. **Vaccination:** Stimulate immunity against specific diseases\n2. **Biosecurity:** Prevent introduction of disease onto farm\n3. **Good hygiene:** Clean housing, water, feed\n4. **Quarantine:** Isolate new or sick animals\n5. **Proper nutrition:** Well-nourished animals resist disease\n6. **Pest control:** Reduce vectors (ticks, flies)\n\n**Treatment:**\n- Antibiotics for bacterial diseases\n- Anthelmintics for worm infections\n- Acaricides for ticks and mites\n- Supportive therapy (fluids, vitamins)\n\n### Zoonotic Diseases\nDiseases transmissible between animals and humans:\n- **Rabies:** Via dog/animal bite\n- **Brucellosis:** Via raw milk, infected animals\n- **Tuberculosis:** Via raw milk from infected cattle\n- **Anthrax:** Via infected animals/products\n- **Leptospirosis:** Via urine of infected animals\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Classify diseases by cause (bacterial, viral, protozoa)\n- Know major diseases of cattle, sheep, goats, and poultry\n- Distinguish ectoparasites from endoparasites\n- Explain control methods for ticks and internal worms\n- List zoonotic diseases and how they spread to humans',
   12, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 13: Fish Farming (Aquaculture)
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000013-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Fish Farming (Aquaculture)',
   'Types of aquaculture, fish pond construction, management and common fish species',
   E'## Fish Farming (Aquaculture)\n\n### Definition\nAquaculture (fish farming) is the controlled cultivation of aquatic organisms including fish, shellfish, and aquatic plants for commercial purposes.\n\n### Importance of Fish Farming\n- Provides animal protein for the population\n- Supplements wild fish captures (overfishing is a global concern)\n- Employment and income generation\n- Year-round production (not seasonal)\n- Foreign exchange from exports\n\n### Types of Aquaculture\n\n**1. Freshwater Aquaculture**\n- Most common in West Africa\n- Fish species: Catfish, Tilapia, Carp, Heterotis\n- Ponds, tanks, cages in rivers and lakes\n\n**2. Marine/Brackishwater Aquaculture**\n- Shrimp, oyster, seaweed farming in coastal areas\n\n**3. Integrated Farming Systems**\n- Fish-cum-rice farming\n- Fish-pig integration (pig effluent fertilizes pond)\n- Fish-poultry integration\n\n### Common Fish Species in West Africa\n\n**Catfish (Clarias gariepinus):**\n- Most popular in Nigeria, Ghana\n- Air-breathing — survives in low oxygen water\n- Fast growth rate\n- Hardy and disease-resistant\n- Market weight: 1–1.5 kg in 6 months\n\n**Tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus):**\n- Second most farmed fish\n- Herbivorous/omnivorous\n- Tolerant of poor water quality\n- Fast growth in warm water\n- Disadvantage: Uncontrolled breeding (overpopulation)\n\n**Mud Fish (Heterotis niloticus):**\n- Indigenous West African species\n- Feeds on plankton and detritus\n\n### Fish Pond Construction\n\n**Types of Fish Ponds:**\n- **Earthen ponds:** Dug into soil — most common, natural\n- **Concrete/plastic tanks:** Indoor or outdoor, easier to manage\n- **Cage culture:** Net cages in rivers, lakes, reservoirs\n- **Race ways (flow-through):** Moving water channels\n\n**Site Selection for Earthen Pond:**\n- Availability of water (river, stream, borehole)\n- Clay soil (holds water, no leakage)\n- Gently sloping land (gravity drainage)\n- Away from flood-prone areas\n- Accessible road\n- Near market\n\n**Pond Design:**\n- **Inlet:** Water supply channel\n- **Outlet/Monk:** Water level control and draining\n- **Embankments (dykes):** Walls retaining water\n- **Freeboard:** 30–50 cm above water level\n- **Depth:** 1–1.5 m for catfish, tilapia\n- **Area:** Variable — from 100 m² to several hectares\n\n### Pond Preparation and Stocking\n\n**1. Liming:**\n- Apply agricultural lime (CaCO₃) to pond bottom\n- Rate: 200–500 kg/ha\n- Raises pH, kills pathogens, improves fertility\n\n**2. Pond Fertilization:**\n- Organic fertilizer (chicken manure, cow dung) to grow plankton\n- Or inorganic fertilizer (NPK)\n- Creates natural food for fish (phytoplankton, zooplankton)\n\n**3. Filling with Water:**\n- Fill through screened inlet to prevent wild fish entry\n- Wait 1–2 weeks for plankton to grow\n\n**4. Stocking:**\n- Select healthy fingerlings (young fish)\n- **Stocking density:** Catfish 3–5/m², Tilapia 2–4/m²\n- Acclimatize fish to pond water temperature\n- Stock in evening to reduce stress\n\n### Feed Management\n\n**Feeding Rate:**\n- 2–5% of body weight per day\n- Reduce as fish grow\n\n**Commercial Fish Feed:**\n- Floating pellets (observe feed conversion)\n- Sinking pellets\n- Protein content: 35–45% for catfish\n\n**Feeding Frequency:**\n- 2–3 times daily\n- Same time and place each day\n\n**Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR):**\n- Amount of feed needed to produce 1 kg of fish\n- Good FCR for catfish: 1.2–1.5\n\n### Water Quality Management\n\n**Key Water Quality Parameters:**\n| Parameter | Optimal Range |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Temperature | 25–30°C |\n| Dissolved Oxygen (DO) | >5 mg/L |\n| pH | 6.5–8.5 |\n| Ammonia | <0.5 mg/L |\n| Transparency | 30–40 cm |\n\n**Management Practices:**\n- Partial water exchange (20–30% weekly)\n- Avoid overfeeding (reduces oxygen)\n- Use aerators if DO is low\n- Regular monitoring\n\n### Harvesting and Marketing\n**Harvest Methods:**\n- Seining (net dragging)\n- Draining pond and collecting fish\n- Partial harvesting with cast nets\n\n**Harvest at:** 6–12 months when fish reach market size\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define aquaculture and explain its importance\n- Know major fish species farmed in West Africa\n- Describe steps in pond construction and preparation\n- Explain stocking, feeding, and water quality management\n- Know the concept of Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR)',
   13, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 14: Agricultural Economics and Farm Management
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000014-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Agricultural Economics and Farm Management',
   'Economic principles, farm planning, record keeping, and farm accounts',
   E'## Agricultural Economics and Farm Management\n\n### Agricultural Economics\nAgricultural economics applies economic principles to the production, distribution, and consumption of agricultural products.\n\n### Farm Management\nFarm management is the process of making decisions on the organization and operation of a farm for maximum profit.\n\n### Factors of Agricultural Production\n\n**1. Land**\n- The physical area used for farming\n- Includes soil, water, forests, minerals\n- Payment: **Rent**\n- Fixed factor of production\n\n**2. Labour**\n- Human effort used in production\n- Types: Family labour, hired labour, contract labour\n- Payment: **Wages**\n- Can be substituted by machinery\n\n**3. Capital**\n- Money and assets used in production\n- Types: Fixed capital (land, machinery), Working capital (seeds, fertilizers)\n- Payment: **Interest**\n\n**4. Management/Entrepreneurship**\n- Skills of organizing and decision-making\n- Payment: **Profit**\n\n### Farm Planning\n**A farm plan** is a written description of how a farmer intends to use their resources to achieve production goals.\n\n**Steps in Farm Planning:**\n1. **Inventory of resources:** Land, labour, capital, equipment\n2. **Set objectives:** Profit, food security, income\n3. **Identify enterprise alternatives:** What to produce\n4. **Analyze alternatives:** Costs and returns\n5. **Select best plan:** Most profitable/sustainable option\n6. **Implement and monitor**\n\n### Farm Records and Accounts\n\n**Importance of Farm Records:**\n- Monitor farm performance\n- Identify profitable and loss-making activities\n- Required for obtaining loans\n- Help in future planning and decision-making\n- Tax purposes\n\n**Types of Farm Records:**\n| Record | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Inventory records | List of assets (land, livestock, machinery) |\n| Cash book | Record of all receipts and payments |\n| Field records | Crop activities, inputs used per field |\n| Labour records | Workers, hours, wages paid |\n| Livestock records | Animal numbers, health, production |\n| Input records | Purchases of seeds, fertilizers, feeds |\n| Sales records | Produce sold, prices received |\n\n### Farm Accounts\n\n**Income Statement (Profit and Loss):**\nCalculates net farm income:\n```\nGross Revenue (Total Sales)\n- Total Variable Costs\n= Gross Margin\n- Fixed Costs\n= Net Farm Income (Profit)\n```\n\n**Types of Costs:**\n\n**Fixed Costs (Overhead):**\n- Do not change with output level\n- Examples: Rent, loan repayment, depreciation, permanent staff salaries\n- Incurred even if nothing is produced\n\n**Variable Costs:**\n- Change with level of production\n- Examples: Seeds, fertilizers, pesticides, hired labour, fuel\n- Zero if nothing is produced\n\n**Total Cost = Fixed Costs + Variable Costs**\n\n**Gross Margin Analysis:**\n- Gross Margin = Gross Revenue - Variable Costs\n- Used to compare profitability of different enterprises\n- Higher gross margin = more profitable enterprise\n\n### Enterprise Analysis\n\n**Enterprise:** A specific crop or livestock activity on the farm\n\n**Gross Margin Calculation Example:**\nFor 1 hectare of maize:\n- Gross Revenue: Yield (2000 kg) × Price (GH₵2/kg) = **GH₵4,000**\n- Variable Costs: Seeds + Fertilizer + Labour + Pesticides = **GH₵2,200**\n- **Gross Margin = GH₵4,000 - GH₵2,200 = GH₵1,800**\n\n### Supply and Demand in Agriculture\n\n**Law of Demand:** As price increases, quantity demanded decreases (inverse relationship)\n**Law of Supply:** As price increases, quantity supplied increases (direct relationship)\n\n**Price Equilibrium:** Price where supply equals demand\n\n**Agricultural Supply Problems:**\n- Seasonal production — glut at harvest, shortage off-season\n- Perishability — quick spoilage causes price collapse\n- Weather risks — drought, flood reduce supply\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know the 4 factors of production and their payments\n- Describe steps in farm planning\n- List and explain types of farm records\n- Distinguish between fixed and variable costs\n- Calculate gross margin for a farm enterprise\n- Explain supply and demand principles in agriculture',
   14, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 15: Agricultural Finance and Marketing
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000015-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Agricultural Finance and Marketing',
   'Sources of agricultural credit, marketing channels, and price determination',
   E'## Agricultural Finance and Marketing\n\n### Agricultural Finance\nAgricultural finance is the provision of credit and financial services to farmers and agribusiness.\n\n### Why Do Farmers Need Credit?\n- To purchase inputs (seeds, fertilizers, pesticides)\n- To buy farm equipment\n- To pay for labour\n- To bridge the gap between planting and harvest income\n- To expand their farm business\n\n### Sources of Agricultural Finance\n\n**1. Formal Sources**\n- **Commercial Banks:** Agricultural development banks (ADB in Ghana)\n- **Rural banks/Community banks:** Local banking for small farmers\n- **Government agricultural funds:** Government subsidies and grants\n- **International organizations:** World Bank, FAO, IFAD loans\n- **Microfinance institutions (MFIs)**\n\n**2. Informal Sources**\n- **Moneylenders:** Local individuals lending at high interest\n- **Family and friends:** No interest, flexible repayment\n- **Rotating Savings Groups (Susu):** Members contribute and take turns\n- **Produce traders:** Advance credit against future crop delivery\n\n**3. Other Sources**\n- Input credit schemes (from agro-chemical companies)\n- Out-grower schemes (companies provide inputs, farmers repay with produce)\n\n### Collateral for Farm Loans\n- **Collateral:** Assets pledged as security for a loan\n- Common collateral: Land documents, farm equipment, livestock\n- Lack of collateral is a major constraint for small farmers in Africa\n\n### Challenges of Agricultural Credit in Africa\n- High interest rates\n- Lack of collateral (no land titles)\n- High risk of agriculture (drought, pests)\n- Banks unwilling to lend to small farmers\n- Poor financial literacy among farmers\n- Remoteness of farms from banks\n\n### Agricultural Marketing\nAgricultural marketing includes all activities involved in moving products from the producer (farmer) to the final consumer.\n\n### Marketing Channels\n\n**Short/Direct Channel:**\n```\nFarmer → Consumer\n```\n- Farmer sells directly at farm gate or farmers\' market\n- Maximum price for farmer, minimum for consumer\n\n**Medium Channel:**\n```\nFarmer → Retailer → Consumer\n```\n\n**Long/Indirect Channel:**\n```\nFarmer → Assembler → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer\n```\n- Most common in West Africa\n- Each middleman adds cost and margin\n\n### Marketing Intermediaries (Middlemen)\n- **Assemblers:** Collect produce from many farmers\n- **Wholesalers:** Buy large quantities, sell to retailers\n- **Retailers:** Sell small quantities to consumers\n- **Processors:** Transform raw produce (gari from cassava)\n- **Exporters:** Market produce internationally\n\n**Role of Middlemen:**\n- Risk bearing\n- Storage and transportation\n- Finance provision\n- Market information\n\n### Marketing Functions\n1. **Buying:** Farmers sell, traders buy\n2. **Selling:** Moving produce to consumers\n3. **Transportation:** Moving from farm to market\n4. **Storage:** Maintaining produce until demand peaks\n5. **Processing:** Value addition (milling, drying, packaging)\n6. **Grading and standardization:** Sorting by quality\n7. **Financing:** Credit in marketing chain\n8. **Risk bearing:** Price and quality risks\n9. **Market intelligence:** Providing price information\n\n### Agricultural Price Formation\n\n**Factors Affecting Farm Prices:**\n- Seasonal availability (prices drop at harvest)\n- Quality and grading\n- Location and distance from market\n- Transportation costs\n- Government price controls/support prices\n- International market prices (for export crops)\n\n**Price Stabilization:**\nGovernments use:\n- **Price floors (minimum prices):** Support prices to protect farmers\n- **Strategic reserves:** Buy at harvest, sell in lean season\n- **Export promotion:** Open new markets\n\n### Agricultural Cooperatives\n**Cooperative:** A voluntary association of people with common economic interests.\n\n**Benefits of Cooperatives:**\n- Bulk buying of inputs at lower prices\n- Collective bargaining for better produce prices\n- Access to credit as a group\n- Shared storage, processing, transport\n- Extension services and training\n\n**Types:**\n- Producer cooperatives\n- Consumer cooperatives\n- Credit cooperatives\n- Marketing cooperatives\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know formal vs informal sources of agricultural finance\n- Draw and explain marketing channels\n- List marketing functions with explanations\n- Explain factors affecting farm gate prices\n- Describe the benefits of agricultural cooperatives',
   15, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 16: Land Tenure and Land Use
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000016-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Land Tenure and Land Use',
   'Systems of land ownership, land use patterns and policies in West Africa',
   E'## Land Tenure and Land Use\n\n### Definition of Land Tenure\nLand tenure refers to the system by which land is owned or held, and the rights and obligations that come with that ownership or use.\n\n### Importance of Land in Agriculture\n- Foundation of all agricultural production\n- Security for loans\n- Determines investment decisions\n- Affects farm productivity and sustainability\n\n### Types of Land Tenure Systems in West Africa\n\n**1. Communal/Customary Land Tenure**\n- Land owned by the community, family, or clan\n- Most widespread system in rural West Africa\n- Chief/family head manages and allocates land\n- Individuals have use rights, not ownership\n- Land cannot usually be sold without community approval\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Prevents landlessness within community\n- Provides land for all community members\n- Culturally accepted\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Insecurity of tenure — land can be taken back\n- Difficult to use as collateral for loans\n- May restrict investment in permanent improvements\n\n**2. Freehold/Individual Ownership**\n- Individual holds full ownership rights\n- Can sell, lease, or use as collateral\n- Registered at Lands Commission\n- More common in urban/peri-urban areas\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Secure tenure — encourages investment\n- Can be used as loan collateral\n- Clear transfer of ownership\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- May exclude poor farmers\n- Land concentration in few hands\n\n**3. Tenancy/Leasehold**\n- Tenant pays rent (money or share of produce) to use land\n- Forms:\n  - **Cash rent:** Fixed money payment\n  - **Sharecropping (Abusa/Abunu system in Ghana):** Farmer gives 1/3 (abusa) or 1/2 (abunu) of produce to landowner\n  - **Contract farming:** Formal lease agreements\n\n**4. Government Land Ownership**\n- State owns large areas (forests, irrigation schemes)\n- Allocated for specific uses\n- Examples: Vea Irrigation Project (Ghana), WARDA rice schemes\n\n**5. Religious Land Ownership**\n- Religious institutions (churches, mosques) own land\n- May be leased to farmers\n\n### Land Fragmentation\nSmall farm sizes due to inheritance subdivision over generations.\n\n**Consequences:**\n- Too small for mechanization\n- Inefficient use of resources\n- Low productivity per farm\n\n**Solutions:**\n- Land consolidation programs\n- Cooperative farming\n- Land leasing\n\n### Land Use Patterns in West Africa\n\n**Agricultural Land Use:**\n| Crop Type | Land Requirement |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| Food crop farming | Small family plots (0.5–2 ha) |\n| Cash crop farming | Medium to large farms |\n| Livestock grazing | Open grasslands, savanna |\n| Plantation agriculture | Large estates (cocoa, rubber, oil palm) |\n\n**Non-Agricultural Land Use:**\n- Forest reserves\n- Game reserves/National parks\n- Urban and industrial development\n- Infrastructure (roads, dams)\n- Mining\n\n### Land Reform\nLand reform refers to changes in laws and policies governing land ownership and use.\n\n**Types:**\n- Redistribution of land from large landowners to landless farmers\n- Land registration and titling programs\n- Tenancy reforms (protect tenant rights)\n\n**Ghana\'s Land Policy:**\n- Land Administration Project (LAP)\n- Customary Land Secretariats\n- Systematic land registration\n\n### Land Degradation\n**Causes:**\n- Overgrazing — compaction, bare soil\n- Deforestation — erosion, loss of fertility\n- Improper farming practices — soil acidification\n- Bush burning — destroys organic matter\n- Overuse without replenishment\n\n**Effects:**\n- Reduced crop yields\n- Desertification (expansion of desert southward in Sahel)\n- Loss of biodiversity\n- Water scarcity\n\n**Solutions:**\n- Sustainable land management (SLM)\n- Agroforestry (planting trees with crops)\n- Soil and water conservation\n- Reforestation\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define land tenure and explain its importance\n- Compare communal vs freehold tenure systems\n- Explain the abusa and abunu sharecropping systems\n- Describe consequences of land fragmentation\n- Know causes and effects of land degradation and solutions',
   16, 25)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 17: Irrigation and Water Management
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000017-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Irrigation and Water Management',
   'Types of irrigation systems, water sources, drainage, and water conservation',
   E'## Irrigation and Water Management\n\n### What is Irrigation?\nIrrigation is the artificial application of water to soil to supplement rainfall for crop production.\n\n### Why is Irrigation Important?\n- Enables year-round crop production\n- Supports crops during dry season\n- Reduces dependence on erratic rainfall\n- Increases crop yields and quality\n- Stabilizes farm income\n\n### Water Sources for Irrigation\n- **Rivers and streams:** Most common source\n- **Lakes and reservoirs/dams:** (e.g., Volta Lake in Ghana)\n- **Groundwater:** Boreholes and wells\n- **Rainwater harvesting:** Collecting runoff in tanks\n- **Municipal water supply:** For urban horticulture\n\n### Methods/Types of Irrigation\n\n**1. Surface Irrigation**\nWater flows over the soil surface by gravity.\n\n**a) Flood/Basin Irrigation:**\n- Water floods entire field\n- Suitable for rice, flat land\n- Cheap to install, high water use\n\n**b) Furrow Irrigation:**\n- Water flows in small channels (furrows) between crop rows\n- Suitable for row crops (maize, vegetables, sugarcane)\n- Less water use than flood irrigation\n\n**c) Border Irrigation:**\n- Field divided into long strips with small borders\n- Water flows along strips\n\n**Advantages of Surface Irrigation:**\n- Low cost, uses gravity\n- No special equipment needed\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- High water use\n- Risk of waterlogging and salinity\n- Labor intensive\n- Not suitable for uneven land\n\n**2. Sprinkler/Overhead Irrigation**\nWater applied through a system of pipes and nozzles/sprinklers.\n\n**Types:**\n- Fixed/Solid set sprinklers\n- Rotary/Impact sprinklers\n- Center pivot irrigation (large circular systems)\n- Traveling gun sprinklers\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Uniform application\n- Suitable for varied topography\n- Can apply fertilizers (fertigation)\n- Less water use than surface irrigation\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- High initial cost\n- Requires pressure and power\n- Wind may affect distribution\n- May promote fungal diseases (wet leaves)\n\n**3. Drip/Trickle Irrigation**\nWater applied slowly and directly to root zone.\n\n**Components:** Pump, main pipe, sub-mains, drip lines, emitters/drippers\n\n**Advantages:**\n- Most water-efficient (90-95% efficiency)\n- Suitable for vegetables and fruits\n- Reduces weed growth\n- Can apply fertilizers precisely\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n- Very high initial cost\n- Emitters can clog\n- Requires filtered, clean water\n- Technical expertise needed\n\n### Water Use Efficiency\n\n**Irrigation Efficiency:**\n- Ratio of water beneficially used to water applied\n- Surface: 40–60%\n- Sprinkler: 60–80%\n- Drip: 90–95%\n\n**Improving Efficiency:**\n- Mulching to reduce evaporation\n- Irrigate in early morning or evening\n- Proper scheduling based on crop water needs\n- Repair leaks in irrigation system\n\n### Crop Water Requirements\n- **Evapotranspiration (ET):** Water lost through evaporation from soil + transpiration from plants\n- Crops require different amounts of water at different growth stages\n- Critical periods: Germination, flowering, grain filling\n\n### Drainage\n**Drainage** is the removal of excess water from the soil.\n\n**Need for Drainage:**\n- Prevents waterlogging\n- Improves soil aeration for root growth\n- Allows timely fieldwork\n- Prevents salinity build-up\n\n**Types of Drainage:**\n- **Surface drainage:** Open channels to carry away surface water\n- **Subsurface drainage:** Underground pipes (tile drains) to lower water table\n- **Mole drainage:** Bullet-shaped implement creates underground channel\n\n### Problems with Irrigation\n\n**1. Waterlogging:**\n- Soil saturated with water\n- Causes root oxygen deficiency\n- Plants wilt and die\n- Solution: Improve drainage\n\n**2. Salinity/Salinization:**\n- Salts accumulate in soil as water evaporates\n- Toxic to most crops\n- Reduces soil fertility\n- Solution: Leaching, drainage, salt-tolerant crops\n\n**3. Alkalinity:**\n- High sodium accumulation\n- Destroys soil structure\n- Solution: Gypsum application, drainage\n\n### Major Irrigation Schemes in West Africa\n- **Kpong Irrigation Project** — Ghana, Volta River\n- **Vea Irrigation Project** — Northern Ghana\n- **Weija Irrigation** — Ghana\n- **Kano River Project** — Nigeria\n- **Office du Niger** — Mali (rice production)\n- **Richard-Toll** — Senegal (rice and sugarcane)\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define irrigation and explain its importance\n- Compare surface, sprinkler, and drip irrigation\n- Know irrigation efficiency percentages for each method\n- Explain waterlogging and salinization problems\n- Describe types of drainage (surface vs subsurface)\n- Name irrigation schemes in West Africa',
   17, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 18: Agricultural Biotechnology
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000018-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Agricultural Biotechnology',
   'Traditional and modern biotechnology, tissue culture, GMOs, and their applications in agriculture',
   E'## Agricultural Biotechnology\n\n### Definition\nBiotechnology is the use of living organisms, biological systems, or their derivatives to develop or modify products or processes for specific use.\n\n**Agricultural Biotechnology** applies biological knowledge and techniques to improve crop and livestock production.\n\n### Traditional Biotechnology\nUsed by farmers for thousands of years:\n- **Selective breeding:** Choosing best-performing plants/animals for reproduction\n- **Fermentation:** Producing beer, bread, cheese, kenkey using microorganisms\n- **Cross-pollination:** Hybridization of crop varieties\n- **Crop improvement through selection**\n\n### Modern Biotechnology Techniques\n\n**1. Tissue Culture (Micropropagation)**\n**Definition:** Growing plant cells, tissues, or organs in a sterile artificial nutrient medium.\n\n**Process:**\n1. Take explant (small piece of plant — leaf, stem, meristem)\n2. Sterilize and place on sterile nutrient agar medium\n3. Cells divide to form callus (undifferentiated mass)\n4. Induce shoot and root differentiation with hormones\n5. Transfer to greenhouse for hardening\n6. Transfer to field\n\n**Advantages of Tissue Culture:**\n- Mass production of disease-free planting material\n- Rapid propagation of rare or valuable plants\n- Year-round production independent of season\n- Propagation of plants that don\'t produce seeds\n- Conservation of endangered species\n\n**Examples in West Africa:**\n- Banana and plantain propagation\n- Oil palm clonal propagation\n- Cassava virus-free planting material\n\n**2. Genetic Engineering (Recombinant DNA Technology)**\nManipulation of an organism\'s genes.\n\n**Process:**\n1. Identify gene of interest\n2. Cut gene using restriction enzymes\n3. Insert into vector (carrier — usually plasmid from bacteria)\n4. Insert vector into target organism\n5. Select transformed organisms\n\n**Applications:**\n- Disease-resistant crops\n- Herbicide-tolerant crops\n- Drought-tolerant crops\n- Improved nutritional quality\n\n**3. Genetically Modified Organisms (GMOs)**\nOrganisms whose genetic material has been altered using genetic engineering.\n\n**Examples of GMO Crops:**\n| Crop | Modification | Benefit |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| Bt maize/cotton | Bacillus thuringiensis gene | Insect resistance |\n| Herbicide-tolerant soybean | Roundup-Ready gene | Weed control |\n| Golden Rice | Beta-carotene gene | Vitamin A source |\n| Virus-resistant cassava | Virus resistance gene | Higher yield |\n\n**Arguments FOR GMOs:**\n- Higher yields\n- Reduced pesticide use\n- Drought and disease resistance\n- Better nutrition (biofortification)\n- Reduced post-harvest losses\n\n**Arguments AGAINST GMOs:**\n- Unknown long-term health effects\n- Environmental risks (gene flow to wild plants)\n- Corporate control of seed supply\n- Reduced biodiversity\n- Ethical concerns\n\n**4. Marker-Assisted Selection (MAS)**\n- Using DNA markers to identify plants/animals with desirable genes\n- Faster than conventional breeding\n- No gene transfer across species\n\n**5. Genomics**\n- Complete mapping of an organism\'s genome\n- Helps identify genes controlling important traits\n\n**6. Animal Cloning**\n- Producing genetically identical copies of an animal\n- Example: Dolly the sheep (first cloned mammal)\n- Potential for multiplying superior livestock\n\n**7. Artificial Insemination (AI)**\n- Collecting semen from superior bulls\n- Storing in liquid nitrogen\n- Inseminating cows artificially\n- Allows one bull to sire thousands of calves\n\n**8. Embryo Transfer**\n- Collecting embryos from genetically superior cow\n- Implanting in surrogate cows\n- One superior cow can produce 10–20 offspring per year\n\n### Biosafety and Regulation\n- **Biosafety:** Measures to prevent harm from biotechnology\n- Ghana has the Biosafety Act (2011) and Ghana Biosafety Authority\n- Environmental and health assessments required before GMO release\n- Public debate and regulatory approval needed\n\n### Bioremediation\n- Using microorganisms to clean up pollution\n- Oil spill cleanup using hydrocarbon-degrading bacteria\n- Heavy metal removal from contaminated soil\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define biotechnology and distinguish traditional from modern\n- Describe tissue culture process and its advantages\n- Explain genetic engineering and give examples of GMO crops\n- List arguments for and against GMOs\n- Know artificial insemination and embryo transfer in livestock',
   18, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 19: Environmental Impact of Agriculture
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000019-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Environmental Impact of Agriculture',
   'Effects of agriculture on the environment and sustainable agricultural practices',
   E'## Environmental Impact of Agriculture\n\n### Agriculture and the Environment\nAgriculture both depends on and affects the natural environment. While agriculture feeds the world, it can also cause significant environmental damage when not managed sustainably.\n\n### Positive Effects of Agriculture on the Environment\n- **Agroforestry:** Trees in farms increase carbon storage\n- **Cover crops:** Protect soil from erosion\n- **Wetland farming:** Can maintain wetland ecosystems\n- **Bee-keeping:** Supports pollinator populations\n- **Organic farming:** Improves soil health and biodiversity\n\n### Negative Environmental Effects of Agriculture\n\n**1. Deforestation**\n- Clearing forests for farmland\n- West Africa has lost over 80% of original forest cover\n- Consequences:\n  - Loss of biodiversity\n  - Reduced rainfall\n  - Soil erosion\n  - Climate change (loss of carbon sink)\n  - Extinction of species\n\n**2. Soil Degradation**\n- Monocropping depletes specific nutrients\n- Overuse without organic matter addition reduces fertility\n- Compaction from heavy machinery\n- Acidification from excessive nitrogen fertilizers\n\n**3. Water Pollution**\n- **Fertilizer runoff:** Nitrates and phosphates enter water bodies\n  - Causes eutrophication (excessive algae growth)\n  - Algal blooms deplete oxygen — fish die\n- **Pesticide contamination:** Kills non-target organisms\n- **Irrigation salinization:** Salt enters groundwater\n- **Animal waste:** Pathogens and nutrients pollute water\n\n**4. Air Pollution**\n- **Methane (CH₄):** From ruminant digestive systems and rice paddies\n- **Nitrous oxide (N₂O):** From soil with nitrogen fertilizers\n- **Ammonia:** From livestock waste — acid rain\n- **Carbon dioxide:** From fuel burning, deforestation\n- **Pesticide drift:** Aerial spraying\n- **Burning crop residues:** Smoke and particulates\n\n**5. Loss of Biodiversity**\n- Monoculture reduces habitat diversity\n- Pesticide use kills beneficial insects, birds, soil organisms\n- Invasive species introduced with crops\n- Genetic erosion — loss of traditional crop varieties\n\n**6. Land Degradation and Desertification**\n- Overgrazing by livestock destroys vegetation\n- Desertification — expansion of desert conditions\n- Particularly severe in Sahel zone (Nigeria, Niger, Mali)\n\n**7. Greenhouse Gases and Climate Change**\nAgriculture contributes about **24%** of global greenhouse gas emissions:\n- Livestock: methane from enteric fermentation\n- Rice cultivation: methane from flooded paddies\n- Fertilizer use: nitrous oxide\n- Land use change (deforestation): CO₂\n\n### Sustainable Agriculture\n**Sustainable agriculture** meets current food needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their needs.\n\n**Principles of Sustainable Agriculture:**\n1. **Maintain soil health:** Use organic matter, minimize tillage\n2. **Water conservation:** Efficient irrigation, rainwater harvesting\n3. **Biodiversity conservation:** Intercropping, agroforestry\n4. **Reduce chemical inputs:** IPM, organic fertilizers\n5. **Minimize waste:** Compost, recycle farm waste\n6. **Economic viability:** Profitable for farmers\n7. **Social equity:** Fair labor practices, community benefit\n\n### Sustainable Practices\n\n**Agroforestry:**\n- Growing trees alongside crops or livestock\n- Trees fix nitrogen, provide shade, conserve water\n- Examples: Parkland agroforestry in Sahel (Faidherbia albida)\n\n**Conservation Agriculture (CA):**\n- Minimum tillage (no-till or reduced till)\n- Permanent soil cover with mulch/crop residues\n- Crop rotation\n- Benefits: Reduces erosion, improves soil, saves fuel\n\n**Organic Farming:**\n- No synthetic chemicals\n- Relies on organic inputs (compost, green manure)\n- Supports soil organisms\n- Premium market prices\n\n**Integrated Pest Management (IPM)**\n- Minimum pesticide use\n- Biological and cultural controls preferred\n\n**Precision Agriculture:**\n- Using GPS, sensors, drones\n- Apply inputs only where needed\n- Reduces waste and environmental impact\n\n**Riparian Buffers:**\n- Vegetated strips along water bodies\n- Prevent runoff and erosion\n- Protect water quality\n\n### Climate Change and Agriculture\n**Effects of Climate Change on Agriculture:**\n- Increased temperatures affect crop development\n- Changed rainfall patterns — droughts and floods\n- New pest and disease distributions\n- Sea level rise affecting coastal farmland\n- Reduced freshwater availability\n\n**Adaptation Strategies:**\n- Drought-resistant crop varieties\n- Adjusting planting calendars\n- Water harvesting\n- Crop diversification\n- Agroforestry\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Know negative effects of agriculture on environment (deforestation, pollution, biodiversity loss)\n- Explain eutrophication from fertilizer runoff\n- Describe sustainable agriculture principles\n- Know practices: agroforestry, conservation agriculture, organic farming\n- Explain how agriculture contributes to climate change\n- Know adaptation strategies for climate change in agriculture',
   19, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- Topic 20: Agricultural Extension and Rural Development
INSERT INTO topics (id, subject_id, name, description, content, order_index, duration_minutes) VALUES
  ('b8000020-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
   'a1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
   'Agricultural Extension and Rural Development',
   'Agricultural extension services, communication methods, rural development programs and policy',
   E'## Agricultural Extension and Rural Development\n\n### Agricultural Extension\n**Definition:** Agricultural extension is the process of transferring knowledge, skills, and technologies from research institutions to farmers to help them improve their agricultural practices and livelihoods.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n- **Extension agent:** Trained professional who works directly with farmers\n- **Extension education:** Non-formal adult education for farmers\n- **Technology transfer:** Moving innovations from research to practice\n- **Adoption:** Farmer acceptance and use of new practices\n\n### Objectives of Agricultural Extension\n1. Transfer improved farming technologies to farmers\n2. Improve farm production and productivity\n3. Enhance farmers\' incomes and standard of living\n4. Train farmers in better farm management\n5. Collect farmers\' problems and feedback for researchers\n6. Empower women and youth in agriculture\n7. Facilitate access to inputs, credit, and markets\n\n### Extension Approaches/Methods\n\n**Individual Methods (One-on-One):**\n- Farm and home visits by extension agent\n- Office calls (farmer visits extension office)\n- Telephone consultations\n- Personal demonstrations\n\n**Advantages:** Direct contact, tailored advice\n**Disadvantages:** Expensive, time-consuming, few farmers reached\n\n**Group Methods:**\n- **Method demonstrations:** Show how to do a new practice\n- **Result demonstrations:** Show results of improved practice vs old\n- **Field days:** Farmers visit successful demonstration plots\n- **Group meetings and seminars**\n- **Farmer field schools (FFS):** Season-long group learning\n\n**Advantages:** Cost-effective, peer learning, more farmers reached\n**Disadvantages:** Requires scheduling, may not suit individual needs\n\n**Mass/Media Methods:**\n- Radio programs (most effective in rural Africa)\n- Television agriculture programs\n- Newspapers and farm magazines\n- Mobile phones and SMS services\n- Internet and social media\n- Posters, leaflets, films\n\n**Advantages:** Reaches large numbers, cost-effective per person\n**Disadvantages:** One-way communication, no individual feedback\n\n### The Adoption and Diffusion Process\n**Adoption** is the mental process a farmer goes through before accepting a new technology.\n\n**Stages of Adoption:**\n1. **Awareness:** Farmer hears about innovation\n2. **Interest:** Seeks more information\n3. **Evaluation:** Considers if it will work for them\n4. **Trial:** Tests on small scale\n5. **Adoption:** Accepts and uses fully\n\n**Categories of Adopters:**\n| Category | % of Farmers | Characteristics |\n|----------|-------------|------------------|\n| Innovators | 2.5% | Risk-takers, educated, wealthy |\n| Early Adopters | 13.5% | Leaders, progressive |\n| Early Majority | 34% | Deliberate, accept before average |\n| Late Majority | 34% | Skeptical, adopt under pressure |\n| Laggards | 16% | Traditional, resistant to change |\n\n**Factors Affecting Technology Adoption:**\n- **Relative advantage:** Better than existing practice?\n- **Compatibility:** Fits local culture and resources?\n- **Complexity:** Easy to understand and use?\n- **Trialability:** Can be tested on small scale?\n- **Observability:** Can results be seen by others?\n\n### Extension Systems in West Africa\n\n**Training and Visit (T&V) System:**\n- Used extensively in Ghana, Nigeria in 1970s-80s\n- Regular training of extension agents\n- Scheduled visits to contact farmers\n\n**Farmer-to-Farmer Extension:**\n- Successful farmers teach neighbors\n- Culturally appropriate and trusted\n\n**National Extension Systems:**\n- **Ghana:** Ministry of Food and Agriculture (MoFA) — Agricultural Extension Directorate\n- **Nigeria:** Agricultural Development Programmes (ADPs)\n\n### Rural Development\n**Rural development** refers to the process of improving the quality of life and economic wellbeing of people living in rural areas.\n\n**Components of Rural Development:**\n- Agricultural development (food security)\n- Infrastructure (roads, electricity, water)\n- Education (rural schools, adult literacy)\n- Healthcare (rural clinics)\n- Financial services (rural banks)\n- Market development\n\n### Gender in Agricultural Extension\n- Women make up 60-80% of food producers in West Africa\n- Traditionally underserved by extension services\n- **Women Extension Agents** increase access to female farmers\n- **Women farmers\' groups** enable collective learning\n\n### ICT in Agricultural Extension\n**Information and Communication Technology (ICT):**\n- Mobile phone apps for price information (M-Farm, Esoko)\n- SMS alerts for weather and market prices\n- Online agricultural advisory services\n- GPS for farm mapping\n- Drones for crop monitoring\n\n### Farmer Organizations\n- **Farmer-Based Organizations (FBOs):** Collective action for inputs and markets\n- **Cooperatives:** Shared resources and collective bargaining\n- **National Farmers Unions:** Political advocacy for farmer interests\n\n### Government Agricultural Policies\nGovernments support agriculture through:\n- Input subsidies (fertilizer, seed subsidies)\n- Price support programs\n- Agricultural research funding (CSIR in Ghana)\n- Extension service funding\n- Irrigation infrastructure\n- Land reform policies\n\n**Ghana Examples:**\n- PLANTING FOR FOOD AND JOBS (PFJ) program\n- Rearing for Food and Jobs\n- Presidential Special Initiatives (PSI)\n\n### WASSCE Key Points\n- Define agricultural extension and state its objectives\n- Compare individual, group, and mass extension methods\n- Describe the 5 stages of technology adoption\n- Explain factors affecting technology adoption\n- Know the categories of adopters with percentages\n- Describe components of rural development\n- Explain the role of ICT in modern agricultural extension',
   20, 30)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
